Phase 7.5 - Reduce the cost of integrating the stored volume into the view
  Depends on: Phase 7. Phase 7.4 is required only for changes sharing its submission ownership;
    Phase 7.3 is not an implementation gate.
  Outcome: the dominant cloud GPU pass has an explicit performance/appearance tradeoff without
    silently changing the selected full-resolution, half-voxel integration contract.
  [ ] Use the captured view cost, viewport and executable plus source review to identify redundant
      arithmetic/fetches, traversal, attenuation, OIT bin calculations and blending work. Profile only
      unresolved costs relevant to the selected change; do not repeat a broad baseline. Confirm
      hardware trilinear filtering, empty-block skipping and compiled opacity termination remain effective.
  [ ] Remove redundant view arithmetic, fetches and state work where the selected integration and OIT
      semantics permit. Preserve depth clipping, finite coverage, physical source/medium composition,
      circular seams and whole-ray failure behavior; do not reintroduce per-sample lighting traces.
  [ ] Evaluate a cheaper occupied integration schedule and reduced-resolution spatial reconstruction
      as explicit alternatives. Quantify pixel/sample savings, reconstruction/composition cost and
      expected edge/thin-layer effects. Do not silently inherit the other renderer's TraceScale.
  [ ] Record the selected design with the user before changing spacing, resolution or defaults; update
      the proposal's numerical/view contract before dependent implementation. Screen-space temporal
      accumulation remains outside the selected design unless separately approved.
  [ ] Implement only the selected alternative through existing abstractions and compiled feature
      options. Extend affected contract checks only, through subagent incremental builds/tests; reuse
      evidence and avoid packaging/full matrices. Use narrow matched GPU measurements and user-run
      appearance review, with no approximation-quality, image-difference or convergence test gates.
  [ ] Completion: view cost and any selected visual tradeoff are documented and measured separately
      from illumination amortization; unchanged-setting savings and changed-setting savings are labeled.

Phase 7.6 - Avoid unnecessary whole-volume diffuse copying
  Depends on: Phases 6 and 7.2; Phase 7.4 only for changed submission ownership.
    Phase 7.3 is not an implementation gate.
  Outcome: copying is proportional to justified publication/transport needs where feasible, without
    losing a coherent old diffuse source or corrupting untouched cells and encoding.
  [ ] Reuse the captured copy dispatch and .16-.18 ms replay timing plus source-derived approximately
      267 MiB read/write traffic per maximum-domain nonempty batch. Separate gather ping-pong copying
      from drawable snapshot publication; measure missing costs only where needed to choose or verify
      the replacement. Do not present this copy as the sole FPS cause or require live attribution first.
  [ ] Compare bounded alternatives for retaining untouched values without a full copy every batch.
      Account for filter-neighbor reads, immutable old-field recurrence, scale changes, memory/metadata,
      replacement and drawable isolation. Record the selected design and reconcile the proposal's
      copy-before-partial-update requirement before changing that contract.
  [ ] Implement only the justified design, or explicitly defer replacement if total cost is worse.
      Preserve empty-work no-copy/no-swap behavior, coherent field/encoding publication, successful-only
      completion stamps and reset/recenter/failure safety; do not add unjustified spare volume storage.
  [ ] Extend affected existing publication/encoding/untouched-region checks only. Use subagents for
      focused incremental validation, reuse evidence and avoid full matrices/packaging. Compare total
      update cost and bytes against the retained baseline, including any new maintenance overhead.
  [ ] Completion: copying is reduced with coherent recurrence/publication, or a measured design decision
      explicitly retains it; memory accounting and current architecture reflect the delivered choice.

